  "costUsd": null,
  "content": "Owen's approach play is the weakest on the team and well behind the Tour. His strokes gained on approach is -4.16 per round over his last ten rounds. Only 8 of those rounds have strokes gained recorded, and they run from June 9 to September 26. That puts him 7th of the 7 players with data, so his place in the team is 0%.\n\n- **Level:** I can't compare him to a Division I average. There is no college figure for strokes gained, so the level comparison is blank.\n- **Team:** The team average is -2.33 over the same window. Owen is below it, and Ethan Park (-4.40) and Cole Bennett (-3.60) are the closest to him. Audit Testplayer has no rounds in the window, so the average covers 7 players.\n- **Tour:** The PGA Tour baseline is 0, so Owen is 4.16 strokes per round below it. Being behind the Tour is the expected picture for a college player, so the team comparison is the more useful one here.\n\nHis greens in regulation are 61.11% over 144 holes in the same window. That is just above the Division I average of 60%, which puts him 36th percentile among 71 college players. It is below the team average of 66.25% and the Tour figure of 66%. His greens-hit rate is near the college level, but his strokes gained on approach is the lowest on the team. That could mean the problem is in the quality of his approach shots rather than in how often he hits the green. I haven't looked at his proximity or miss patterns, so that is only a hypothesis.\n\nHis all-history figure of -3.83 comes from a different window, and the team average there was also -2.33. I used the last-ten figure above.",
